[433,0,0,1886,"Marie-Eugène Chevreul at 100","The chemist Michel-Eugène Chevreul turned a hundred in 1886, and Paul Nadar, Nadar's son, photographed him as he talked. The pictures were published with his words in a newspaper, the first photo-interview ever.","What would you ask someone who is a hundred years old?"],
[434,0,0,1886,"The First Photo-Interview","Paul Nadar's series of Chevreul gesturing and speaking was printed with captions of his words. It was the first time a newspaper used photographs to tell an interview, and it is the grandfather of today's video interviews.","Which of his expressions do you like best?"],
[443,1,0,1887,"November Days","A road lined with bare trees runs into the distance on a cold day. Alfred Stieglitz was a young student in Germany when he made this quiet picture and began to love photography.","What season does it feel like?"],
