[449,7,0,1887,"A Buffalo Walking","This plate from Animal Locomotion shows a bison walking, step by step. Muybridge photographed animals at the Philadelphia Zoo, and artists and scientists used his plates to study how bodies move.","How does the buffalo's head move as it walks?"],
[452,0,0,1888,"The Stieglitz Family at Oaklawn","A big family gathers on a porch and steps at Lake George, New York. Alfred Stieglitz's family owned a summer home there, and it later became his favorite place to photograph.","How many people can you count?"],
[453,2,0,1888,"The Eiffel Tower Under Construction","Louis-Émile Durandelle photographed the Eiffel Tower as its iron legs rose above Paris. It was finished in March 1889 and was the tallest structure in the world, at about 300 meters.","How many levels does the tower have so far?"],
[457,3,0,1888,"Bandits' Roost","Jacob Riis photographed a narrow alley in New York's Mulberry Bend, one of the roughest slums in the city. He used flash powder, which produced a sudden bang and light, so people in his pictures often look startled.","What do you notice about the people's faces?"],
[459,3,0,1888,"Baby in a Slum Tenement Hallway","A baby crawls on the floor in a hallway of a crowded New York tenement. Riis's pictures showed readers that children were growing up in dark, cramped buildings.","What would you change about this hallway?"],
[460,8,0,1889,"Sun Rays — Paula, Berlin","Alfred Stieglitz photographed a young woman writing at a table beside a window, with the sunlight forming stripes. It was one of his first pictures to win notice, and it shows that a photographer can paint with light.","Where does the sunlight fall in the room?"],
[462,3,0,1889,"Five Cents a Spot","In this crowded tenement room in New York's Bayard Street, lodgers paid five cents a night for a spot to sleep on the floor. Jacob Riis made the picture with flash powder, and it helped convince New Yorkers to improve housing.","How many people can you spot sleeping in the room?"],
[469,3,0,1889,"Bohemian Cigar Makers at Work","Families from Bohemia (now part of the Czech Republic) rolled cigars at home in their tiny New York apartments, working for very little pay. Riis photographed them in their cramped kitchen workroom.","What would it be like to work where you live?"],
[470,3,0,1889,"Sweatshop in a Ludlow Street Tenement","Men, women and children sewed clothes in crowded rooms, often for long hours. Riis's pictures of sweatshops showed the hidden work behind the clothes people bought.","What can you tell about their jobs from the room?"],
[471,3,0,1889,"Family in Poverty Gap","A family gathers in a dark basement room in a poor corner of New York called Poverty Gap. Jacob Riis wrote that the room was damp and dim, and that children lived there year-round.","What would you want for this family?"],
